  "query_preview": "..\n\n-  Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue with the persistent\n   weather pattern through Friday.\n\n-  Isolated dry thunderstorm potential: West-central Montana this\n   afternoon.\n\n-  Elevated winds and fire weather concerns: This weekend, region-wide.\n\nVariable air quality remains the primary short-term impact across\nthe region as a dry, hot southwesterly flow persists over the \nNorthern Rockies. This pattern will hold through Friday before a \nPacific trough moves onshore Saturday, breaking down the dominant \nupper-level ridge of high pressure. Historically, synoptic ridge \nbreakdowns following an extended period of hot, dry weather \nsignificantly elevate fire weather concerns.\n\nMonsoon moisture will bring showers a little further north than\nyesterday, including areas south and east of Missoula. Gusty \noutflow winds of 40-50 mph will be common near any thunderstorms \nwith a marginal chance (5%) of severe gusts over 60 mph. Dry \nlightning ignitions will be an additional hazard. The latest high\nresolution models depict the higher probability for lightning\nexists from Sula north and east to Maxville. \n\nSaturday through early next week: Winds will increase on Saturday\nas the main Pacific trough advances eastward. The strongest winds\nwill accompany and follow a dry cold-frontal passage Saturday\nafternoon into Sunday, with widespread westerly winds sustained \nat 10 to 25 mph and gusts reaching 25 to 40 mph across the region.\nThe combination of gusty winds, persistent low relative humidity,\nand dry fuels will create critical fire weather conditions, \naccordingly fire weather watches have been issued. Additionally, \nwinds will produce hazardous, choppy water on area lakes and \nrivers.\n\nThis system will remain predominantly dry with no significant \nprecipitation expected. However, post-frontal cold air advection \nwill drop temperatures 10 to 20 degrees for Sunday and Monday, \nreturning high temperatures to slightly below seasonal averages.\nIf you're planning on camping in the higher elevations Sunday or\nMonday night, be prepared for possible lows in the 30s! There is a\n50% chance for lows in the upper 30s at Georgetown Lake, MT and\nElk City, Idaho by Tuesday.",
